WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.277
NARRATOR: Suppose a motor should hold one
thousand revolutions per minute, but the

2
00:00:04.277 --> 00:00:07.902
sensor reports nine hundred. NARRATOR: The
controller does not command a correction from

3
00:00:07.902 --> 00:00:08.738
the target alone.

4
00:00:08.738 --> 00:00:13.495
NARRATOR: It first computes tracking error:
the desired output minus the measured output.

5
00:00:13.495 --> 00:00:17.339
NARRATOR: Here, the error is positive one
hundred revolutions per minute. NARRATOR: The

6
00:00:17.339 --> 00:00:19.705
proportional term reacts to the error right
now.

7
00:00:19.705 --> 00:00:25.052
NARRATOR: The integral term accumulates error
over time, helping remove a persistent offset.

8
00:00:25.052 --> 00:00:29.750
NARRATOR: The derivative term responds to how
quickly error is changing, which can

9
00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:31.557
anticipate motion and reduce overshoot.

10
00:00:31.557 --> 00:00:35.469
NARRATOR: Their weighted sum becomes the
control input sent to the system.

11
00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:39.640
NARRATOR: After the motor responds, the sensor
measures again and the loop repeats.

12
00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:45.066
NARRATOR: Larger gains are not automatically
better: poor tuning can create overshoot,

13
00:00:45.066 --> 00:00:46.874
noise sensitivity, or oscillation.

14
00:00:46.874 --> 00:00:51.450
NARRATOR: Engineers start with performance
requirements, tune deliberately, and keep the

15
00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:54.362
controller as simple as the application
allows.

16
00:00:54.362 --> 00:00:58.818
NARRATOR: A PI controller may be enough when
derivative action adds little value.
